{
  "id": "proofs.divisibility.divides-sum",
  "course": "proofs",
  "topic": "divisibility",
  "kind": "divisibility_proof",
  "title": "Divisibility of a sum",
  "prompt": "Let $a$, $b$, $c$ be integers. Prove: if $a \\mid b$ and $a \\mid c$, then $a \\mid (b + c)$.",
  "answer_format": "A structured proof: let, case, expr and witness steps (notes optional). No final answer box.",
  "difficulty": 1,
  "tags": [
    "direct proof",
    "divisibility"
  ],
  "data": {
    "variables": [
      "a",
      "b",
      "c"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      {
        "var": "b",
        "divisor": "a"
      },
      {
        "var": "c",
        "divisor": "a"
      }
    ],
    "target": "b + c",
    "goal": {
      "divisor": "a"
    }
  },
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "a | b means b = a·(some integer). Write b and c that way, with different new letters.",
    "Factor a out of b + c."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "Since $a \\mid b$ and $a \\mid c$, $b = aj$ and $c = ak$ for some integers $j, k$.",
    "Then $b + c = aj + ak = a(j + k)$, and $j + k$ is an integer, so $a \\mid (b + c)$. $\\blacksquare$"
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "sundstrom-mr3",
    "section": "3.1 Direct Proofs",
    "note": "Original problem; see the definition of divides."
  },
  "review": {
    "status": "retired",
    "notes": "Invented problem, retired 2026-11-06: MATH 123 proofs now map 1:1 to Book of Proof exercises."
  },
  "examples": [
    {
      "name": "factor out a",
      "submission": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "kind": "let",
            "content": "b = aj"
          },
